{"name":"Glowing Lantern","hex":"#fbb736","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"glowing","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of glow"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The action of glowing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glowing"},{"term":"lantern","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A case of translucent or transparent material made to protect a flame, or light, used to illuminate its surroundings.","Especially, a metal casing with lens used to illuminate a stage (e.g. spotlight, floodlight)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To furnish with a lantern."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lantern"}],"entries":[{"title":"The Mother Hips discography","blocks":[{"text":"The Mother Hips discography is a list of official studio albums, live albums, EPs, singles, compilations, videos and other recordings released by the band the Mother Hips. The Mother Hips are a rock band based in the San Francisco Bay Area, consisting of members Tim Bluhm, Greg Loiacono, John Hofer, and Brian Rashap. Past members included Isaac Parsons, Mike Wofchuck, Paul Hoaglin and Scott Thunes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Mother_Hips_GAMH_in_SF_Dec_2010.jpg/330px-Mother_Hips_GAMH_in_SF_Dec_2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Mother_Hips_discography"},{"title":"Phosphorescence","blocks":[{"text":"Phosphorescence is a type of photoluminescence related to fluorescence. When exposed to light (radiation) of a shorter threshold wavelength, a phosphorescent substance will glow, absorbing the light and reemitting it at a longer wavelength. Unlike fluorescence, a phosphorescent material does not immediately reemit the radiation it absorbs. Instead, a phosphorescent material absorbs some of the radiation energy and reemits it for a much longer time after the radiation source is removed."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Phosphorescence.jpg/330px-Phosphorescence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phosphorescence"},{"title":"Lantern","blocks":[{"text":"A lantern is a source of lighting, often portable. It typically features a protective enclosure for the light source – historically usually a candle, a wick in oil, or a heated mesh, and often a battery-powered light in modern times – to make it easier to carry and hang up, and make it more reliable outdoors or in drafty interiors. Lanterns may also be used for signaling, as torches, or as general light-sources outdoors."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/CNW_brakeman%27s_kerosene_lantern.JPG/330px-CNW_brakeman%27s_kerosene_lantern.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lantern"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Phosphorescent.jpg/500px-Phosphorescent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Phosphorescent.jpg/1280px-Phosphorescent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Phosphorescent.jpg/500px-Phosphorescent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Phosphorescent, europium-doped, strontium silicate-aluminate oxide powder under visible light, fluorescing/phosphorescing under long-wave UV light, and persistently phosphorescing in total darkness","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhosphorescent.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/JablonskiSimple.png/500px-JablonskiSimple.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/JablonskiSimple.png/500px-JablonskiSimple.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Jablonski diagram of an energy scheme used to explain the difference between fluorescence and phosphorescence. The excitation of molecule A to its singlet excited state (1A*) may, after a short time between absorption and emission (fluorescence lifetime), return immediately to ground state, giving off a photon via fluorescence (decay time). However, sustained excitation is followed by intersystem crossing to the triplet state (3A) that relaxes to the ground state by phosphorescence with much longer decay times.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJablonskiSimple.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png/500px-Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png/1280px-Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png/500px-Electronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"After an electron absorbs a photon of high energy, it may undergo vibrational relaxations and intersystem crossing to another spin state.  Again the system relaxes vibrationally in the new spin state and eventually emits light by phosphorescence.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AElectronic_Processes_Involving_Light.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg/500px-Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg/1280px-Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg/500px-Fused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An extremely intense pulse of short-wave UV light in a flashtube produced this blue persistent-phosphorescence in the amorphous, fused silica envelope, lasting as long as 20 minutes after the 3.5 microsecond flash.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFused_silica_phosphorescence_from_a_24_million_watt_flash.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/MoS2_vacancies.jpg/500px-MoS2_vacancies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/MoS2_vacancies.jpg/500px-MoS2_vacancies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An electron microscope reveals vacancy defects in a crystalline lattice of molybdenum disulfide. The missing sulfur atoms leave dangling bonds between the molybdenum atoms, creating traps in the empty spaces.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoS2_vacancies.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Phosphorescent_pigments%2C_in_light%2C_in_dark%2C_after_4_min_-_zinc_sulfide_and_strontium_aluminate.jpg/120px-Phosphorescent_pigments%2C_in_light%2C_in_dark%2C_after_4_min_-_zinc_sulfide_and_strontium_aluminate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Phosphorescent_pigments%2C_in_ligh